{"title":"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be a triangle and let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoints of sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be chosen strictly inside triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/1/5b1a0f691e308462e4fe9b0476508ef0b065423c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BMC$\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d89d48d3a98b680786ac9610fb170be757efa8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BNC$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" />, respectively, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> lies strictly inside triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/6/25697024d81a441f727e279d2216eeb169abced9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABL$\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> lies strictly inside triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64f7934b5d8a33ac82751385e6b510779e126ccc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />. Suppose that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/7/ff74314fa0a661df96136e3d8549fd8bebf164b8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle KBA = \\angle ACL, \\quad \\angle LBK = \\angle LNC, \\quad \\angle LCK = \\angle BMK.\\]\" width=\"467\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the circumcentre of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7439149f594e9b8f42ef995654a5cf9d644b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKL$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. Prove that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/b/8bb10d6a438df5c7f083e8f5b8c53c9d48538983.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[OM = ON.\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/QDmEhFgBb8M\">https://youtu.be/QDmEhFgBb8M</a> (by Dedekind Cuts)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2026imoq2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/3c/2026imoq2.png/550px-2026imoq2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"440\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/3c/2026imoq2.png/825px-2026imoq2.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/3/3c/2026imoq2.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the circumcentre of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a718b55cf3d3cf2aa67b60f9a86c3bc135b584d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AMN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p><b>Lemma 1</b>: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/d/d2d3e94c303283ba12f344b2475b9b56e62e828c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN \\parallel KL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"86\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p><b>Proof of the Lemma 1:</b>\n</p><p>By definition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/7/5d7ec82a470a832c2686ed2c18a7c131edaee50f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OK = OL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />. Let the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.  Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on the perpendicular line through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. Also, point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> lies on the perpendicular line through the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, of line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />. Note that then the intersections of both lines upon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/b/abb5588023cfa1ac14643e9778699f03eecc57a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> respectively, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/b/d6b93b430668a0187045e5e1462b436c3f3e4695.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HIJF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> is a rectangle. Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/d/d2d3e94c303283ba12f344b2475b9b56e62e828c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN \\parallel KL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"86\" height=\"20\" />, as desired.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are collinear along the perpendicular bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 2:</b>\n</p><p>We prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> share a common perpendicular bisector.\n</p><p>Note that the two circles who circumscribe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/a/90aa302feed3983e7156a34b821ad877642919b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AMN$\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7439149f594e9b8f42ef995654a5cf9d644b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKL$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> respectively, are common at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Also, as their bases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> are parallel by <b>Lemma 1</b>, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a849516f86451d86b411fe01e1c931f551f2c904.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MKNL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" /> is a trapezoid through homothety about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. By definition, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/e/e5e29d74aa8d3a27b7444d0ee4cc026fc4931d30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MNCB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" /> is also a trapezoid. Funny enough, the facts <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/e/a9e023221b64b75b617fa00b83f4c06ca8172b27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle KBA = \\angle ACL, \\angle LBK = \\angle LNC,  \\angle LCK = \\angle BMK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"434\" height=\"18\" /> along with the fact <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a849516f86451d86b411fe01e1c931f551f2c904.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MKNL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" /> is a trapezoid completes the topmost angle measures of the original trapezoid, hence it is given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/f/5afaa36588b307cb304631ae56bd68d1e754943c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MNCB \\sim MKNL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"157\" height=\"14\" />. Extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/0/350503b4142c19abeb151a93622b4de560e27171.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MK$\" width=\"36\" height=\"12\" /> past <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/b/beb5291b88ed134fe5f0f382c4301ba1686f2d77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$NL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> past <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> to intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> respectively. Angle chasing given the constraints of the problem shows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a56dbbf633e79aec50b9a450380b81661aa803c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MKNL \\sim MQRN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"156\" height=\"18\" />. This result could elegantly be obtained through a homothety on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> along <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />, which is the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. However, both trapezoids also share a base, hence they must also share a similar perpendicular bisector, which indeed proves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are collinear along the bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><b>2026 IMO Q2</b>\n</p><p>Given <b>Lemma 2</b> it is obvious that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccb1531d05e595cb65612f16e401bef91ae9480f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle MHD = \\angle MHO = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"204\" height=\"15\" />. Given the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint, it follows through converse the definition of isosceles triangles that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/4/804b73d392f6dd561b212c9c58cc1844a55c3b31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OM = ON$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"91\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>For <b>Lemma 2</b>, it is also possible to show the trapezoids must share a common perpendicular bisector by the mutual power of points and Miquel configurations associated with the three given angle equations. It is also possible to apply Radical Axis Theorem to skip the purpose of Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 entirely and elegantly obtain the exact result without the angle chasing brute force of doom in this solution. That solution is dedicated to @dedekindcuts (video solution)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competitions_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competitions resources\">Mathematics competitions resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"